  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness—specifically preferring more right-skewed lotteries (those with higher probability on worse outcomes). However, the individual does not consider the shape of the distribution or skewness at all. Instead, they focus on expected value and the presence of certainty in Lottery A. While Lottery A could be interpreted as more 'right-skewed' in a degenerate sense (all probability on one outcome), the individual does not reason in these terms. Their justification is based on risk aversion and the appeal of a guaranteed outcome, not on the probabilistic structure of favorable vs. unfavorable outcomes. There is no alignment between the information used by the individual and the information required by the skewness reason, nor between their justification and the rule of the reason. Therefore, it is unlikely that the skewness-based reason was used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
